\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

A power analysis using empirical MMLU IRT parameters confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80, FPR of 0.036--0.042, and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$. Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold.

\subsection{Temporal Comparability: 31\% Item Instability}
\label{sec:temporal}

Comparing 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models, 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$). Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 items (31.32\% $\to$ 31.30\%), because the dual ETS threshold on effect size and significance already provides implicit multiple-testing protection.

Three controls confirm this reflects genuine temporal change. A random split of the 2023 cohort yields only 0.18\% ETS~C items (sampling noise), and an ability-quintile comparison within the same temporal cohort yields 2.9\% (ability differences alone). A within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\ Llama-3) produces 61.3\% ETS~C items, elevated by large ability gaps that confound the DIF signal~\citep{holland1988mh}. The contrast between 2.9\% (ability-controlled) and 61.3\% (ability-confounded) brackets the temporal rate of 31.3\%.

Across all 57 MMLU subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% (computer security) to 48.4\% (business ethics) with unweighted mean $\approx 30\%$. Within-subject matching on 5 representative subjects yields a mean of 38.1\% (range: 31--45\%), reported in \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape} as a robustness check for the matching protocol. Despite an ecological correlation of $\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$) between subject-level C\% and accuracy improvement, item-level logistic regression reveals that domain accuracy improvement explains only 2.4 percentage points of 31.3\% C\% (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$; counterfactual C\% $= 29.0\%$). Thus 92.4\% of the instability reflects item-specific changes unrelated to aggregate domain-level gains. The qualitative conclusion is robust across ETS thresholds: C\% $= 48.9\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.0$, $18.3\%$ at $\geq 2.0$, and $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$. \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape} summarizes the DIF landscape across all cohort comparisons.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{DIF landscape across cohort definitions. ETS~C denotes the percentage of items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$. FPR is measured against Li et al.\ web-overlap labels on the ``clean'' (non-overlapping) subset. Enrichment is the ratio of observed to expected overlap between DIF flags and web-overlap labels.}
\label{tab:temporal_landscape}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Cohort Definition & $N_{\mathrm{ref}}$ / $N_{\mathrm{focal}}$ & ETS~C (\%) & FPR (Li clean) & Enrichment \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024) & 1,080 / 807 & 31.3 & 0.318 & 0.96 \\
Ability quintile (Q1--2 vs.\ Q4--5) & ${\sim}$2,600 / ${\sim}$2,600 & 2.9 & 0.027 & 1.19 \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ 3) & 161 / 308 & 61.3 & -- & -- \\
Random split (sanity) & 540 / 540 & 0.18 & -- & -- \\
Within-subject (avg, 5 subjects) & \textit{varies} & 38.1 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

The enrichment ratio of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} is 0.96 (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}), indistinguishable from chance. This null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$, ${+}0.674$ at $p = 1.0$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). An item can be globally contaminated yet function identically if all cohorts have comparable access. The Li et al.\ labels are the wrong ground truth for a differential method: the near-chance enrichment is expected, not evidence of failure.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF detects differential contamination when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

Three progressively refined approaches (matched-ability quintiles, IRT $\theta$-conditioning, within-subject DIF) all fail to reduce the temporal FPR below 0.31. $\theta$-conditioning achieves $r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$ yet FPR $= 0.312$, and within-subject C\% $= 38.1\%$ exceeds the 25\% bifactor viability threshold. Yen's Q3 diagnostic reveals mild local dependence within subjects (11.3\% of pairs exceed $|Q3| > 0.20$, vs.\ 2.2\% cross-subject; $p = 2.3 \times 10^{-9}$), but cross-subject matching addresses this bias and temporal C\% persists. \Cref{app:structural} reports full results. As a further robustness check, logistic regression DIF on 9 MMLU subjects (1,787 items) confirms 92.1\% concordance between MH ETS~C and LR uniform DIF classifications. An additional 38.7\% of items exhibit non-uniform DIF (ability-by-group interaction; median $|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| = 0.58$), and 29.8\% of MH~A/B items show non-uniform effects that the MH procedure does not detect. The 31\% uniform DIF estimate is therefore conservative.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Framework Validation}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

The critical methodological finding is that the choice of matching strategy determines validity. Cross-subject matching computes the conditioning variable from subjects \emph{other than} the one being tested, achieving $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU subjects while four of five produce $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$. Within-subject matching inflates FPR by ${+}0.186$ on average because the matching variable absorbs part of the injection signal. We adopt cross-subject matching as the standard protocol (\Cref{app:fig_matching}).

\subsection{Downstream Impact}
\label{sec:downstream}

Spearman correlation between full and stable-item rankings is $\rho \approx 0.997$: aggregate model ordering is preserved despite 31\% item instability. DIF-flagged items nonetheless carry more discriminative signal than average ($z = -8$ to $-49$ vs.\ random removal across 21 conditions), though a circularity ablation shows DIF-based removal is the \emph{least} disruptive among non-random strategies (Jaccard overlap with high-discrimination items is only 0.12). Individual model families show distinctive DIF profiles: Phi-3 and Llama-3 gain ${+}700$ to ${+}850$ rank positions under stable-item evaluation, while Mistral-based merges lose $-400$ to $-587$ positions (\Cref{app:fig_downstream}). The DIF instability pattern extends beyond MMLU; \Cref{sec:gsm8k} presents a full replication on GSM8K.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

Replication on GSM8K (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models) yields temporal C\% $= 18.9\%$ (sanity check: 0.3\%), with semi-synthetic $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.818$ at $p = 0.7$ (\Cref{app:gsm8k_details}). The GSM1K held-out set~\citep{zhang2024gsm1k} provides independent contamination validation: for $N = 33$ models, the correlation between contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage is $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$; partial $r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$ after controlling for accuracy). This \emph{negative} correlation contradicts the hypothesis that DIF items benefit contaminated models, and instead suggests that DIF captures genuine capability change distinct from memorization (\Cref{fig:gsm1k}). The result is suggestive at $N = 33$ and warrants replication.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_5_gsm1k_dif_correlation.pdf}
\caption{GSM1K contamination gap vs.\ DIF ranking advantage ($N = 33$). The negative correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$) indicates that models with larger contamination gaps benefit less from DIF-flagged items.}
\label{fig:gsm1k}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Implications for Benchmark Temporal Comparability}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

The 31\% ETS~C instability rate is robust across thresholds (10--49\% at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \in [2.5, 1.0]$) and confirmed by three structural controls (\Cref{sec:structural}). Domain-level accuracy improvement explains only 2.4 percentage points of 31.3\% C\% (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); 92.4\% of the instability is item-specific and unrelated to aggregate capability gains. This instability does not invalidate MMLU as a ranking instrument ($\rho \approx 0.997$ between full and stable-item rankings) but signals that item-level measurement properties are drifting across model generations. Benchmark designers should monitor DIF as a quality diagnostic: an item pool where C\% grows over successive cohorts indicates measurement drift even when rank ordering remains intact.

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:disc_groundtruth}

The near-chance enrichment (0.96) between DIF flags and web-overlap labels exposes a gap that extends beyond our analysis: all prior work validating item-level contamination detectors against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels conflates global exposure with differential functioning. Most MMLU items appear in web text, so web-overlap labels identify a condition shared by nearly all items rather than one that differentiates model cohorts. The enrichment of $\approx 1.0$ is the expected outcome when a differential method is evaluated against a global-exposure reference, not evidence of failure. The field needs validation methodologies that match the detection granularity: item-level differential detectors require item-level differential ground truth.

\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}
\label{sec:disc_diagnostic}

The GSM1K correlation ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$; \Cref{sec:gsm8k}) provides evidence that DIF-flagged items capture behavioral change distinct from memorization. Models with larger contamination gaps benefit \emph{less} from DIF-C items. Of the 249 DIF-C items, 171 favor the focal group ($\alpha_{\mathrm{MH}} < 1$), meaning these items became easier for 2024 models relative to ability-matched 2023 models. The negative correlation with contamination gap concentrates in this focal-favoring subset ($r = -0.347$, $p = 0.048$). Partial correlation ($r = -0.385$, $p = 0.027$) after controlling for accuracy confirms the effect is not an artifact of general model quality. Caveats include a wide confidence interval (95\% CI $= [-0.71, -0.16]$) and limited sample size.

Three structural constraints limit DIF applicability in LLM benchmarks: (1) MMLU's multi-domain structure produces mild local dependence that elevates within-subject DIF rates (\Cref{sec:structural}); (2) reliable detection requires $N \geq 80$ per cohort and $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$; and (3) ability differences between temporal cohorts confound the DIF signal (within-family C\% $= 61.3\%$). A fourth limitation is that MH detects only uniform DIF; logistic regression analysis reveals that 29.8\% of MH~A/B items exhibit non-uniform DIF (\Cref{sec:structural}), making the 31\% estimate conservative rather than exhaustive. Higher-level methods (MIMIC models, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address these constraints.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:disc_limitations}

Key limitations include the inability to distinguish contamination from legitimate capability change, the gap between our idealized injection model and real-world contamination mechanisms, and limited benchmark scope (MMLU + GSM8K). Full limitations appear in \Cref{app:limitations}. Overall, DIF should be understood as a diagnostic lens for what changed across model generations, not a quality filter. LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring alongside score reporting.
